  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 325 AM CDT Sat Sep 26 2026\n\nEarly this morning, a broad upper-level ridge sits over the region\nwhile a short-wave trough approaches from the west. Moisture is\nadvecting into north-central Minnesota this morning ahead of\nthat incoming short-wave. The combination of the frontal forcing\nand sufficient moisture in place later this morning creates a \nday of light to moderate rainfall amounts for north-central \nMinnesota. As this system pushes eastward, it will provide light\nrainfall for northeast Minnesota this evening and early \novernight. Meanwhile, very little rainfall is expected for the \neastern Arrowhead and northwest Wisconsin, with all \nprecipitation ending last Sunday morning. Gusty southeast winds\nwill mix down today to create gusts of 25 mph for the Brainerd\nLakes to Koochiching County region. Expect high temperatures\ntoday to only peak around 60 F for the Brainerd Lakes given that\nearlier onset of precipitation, meanwhile eastward highs will \nrange from the lower 60s in the west to the low 70s in northwest\nWisconsin. Northeast winds over Lake Superior will funnel into \nthe Twin Ports, bringing dangerous rip currents to area beaches \nby this afternoon. Another Beach Hazards Statement is in effect\ntoday for the Lake Superior beaches in the Twin Ports.\n\nEarly Sunday morning, the rain will depart the eastern \nArrowhead and northwest Wisconsin as the short-wave trough \nlifts into Ontario. The rest of the Northland will see skies \ngradually clear from west to east. Temperatures will generally \nrange from the middle 60s to near 70 degrees. Areas in the east \nmay remain a bit cooler due to the lingering morning clouds and \nearlier rain showers. Winds will weaken and shift to the south \nand southwest throughout the day.\n\nThe next round of even more widespready moderate rainfall is\nforecast late Monday into Tuesday. An upper-level deep Western\nCONUS trough will aid in moisture advection from the eastern\nPacific into the Upper Midwest and interact with a shortwave \nmoving through the southern Canadian Plains. This will set the \nstage for a widespread, soaking rainfall across the Northland \nstarting late Monday night and continuing through much of \nTuesday. Heaviest rainfall amounts are currently forecast for \nmainly northern Minnesota for this early next week rainfall, but\nnorthwest Wisconsin still has a 60-80% chance for a light to \nmoderate rainfall event. Rumbles of thunder within the\nwidespread wetting rainfall cannot be ruled out either, but\ndiscrete strong thunderstorms are not expected.\n\nOn Wednesday, the deepened Canadian shortwave will move \neastward and place more zonal flow over the Upper Midwest. This \nwill create a short-lived increase in high temperatures on \nwarmer air advection back over northern Minnesota, still cooler \nin northwest Wisconsin, for Wednesday daytime. By Thursday, a\ncold front moves through the Upper Midwest to Manitoba/northwest\nOntario region. Expect gusty westerly winds Thursday and a \nquick return to more fall-like temperatures in the lower-60s to \nend the upcoming work week. The next frost and freeze potential \nreturns next weekend as the cooler, but more normal for the \nseason, airmass sets up under strong high pressure.",
